          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I get heartburn most evenings after dinner. Is that worth mentioning to a doctor?",
          "answer": "Yes, it is reasonable to mention this to a doctor. Heartburn that happens regularly is one of the most common reasons people see a clinician about their digestion. Nothing in your description matched the emergency warning list I checked. That is not a clearance, so if your symptoms become severe, get worse, or come with new and unexplained changes, seek medical advice promptly.\n\nAccording to a gastroenterology society guideline (GERD: empiric initial management and the alarm features that change it), for adults with typical symptoms such as heartburn and no alarm features, a common first step is a trial of acid-reducing treatment taken before a meal, rather than tests first. The guideline also notes that timing matters. A dose taken with or after food may work less well. Your doctor can decide whether that approach suits you. I can't advise on specific medicines or doses.\n\nA lifestyle reference (Eating with reflux: meal size, timing, and finding individual triggers) says a heavy late dinner is a common trigger, because fatty meals slow stomach emptying and keep food in the stomach longer. It also says alcohol and carbonated drinks can worsen reflux. Many people find two or three personal triggers by keeping a symptom and food diary for a couple of weeks. Bringing that diary to an appointment can help.\n\nThe material I could access did not include the full list of alarm features from the guideline, so I can't tell you which warning signs would change the approach. Ask your clinician which signs they want you to watch for. The material also doesn't address whether your symptoms are caused by reflux, since only a clinician can assess that.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
